<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<network-security-config>
    <!--
      [#238] Cleartext is permitted app-wide, intentionally, because the never-pinned
      fallback reaches the child by LAN IP over http://<child-ip>:11000 (snapshot + web
      console). Android's network-security-config <domain> rules match DNS hostnames only —
      they CANNOT express IP / CIDR ranges — so there is no config-level way to scope
      cleartext to RFC1918 addresses connected to by raw IP. The real transport decision is
      enforced in-app by TransportPolicy (#203): once a child has ever presented a TLS pin,
      the parent refuses to downgrade to cleartext (fails closed), and never-pinned
      connections surface a user-visible insecure-transport warning.

      The previous <domain-config> block listed IP literals (192.168.0.0, 10.0.0.0, …) as
      <domain> entries. Those match nothing (they are not hostnames), so they provided a
      false impression of LAN-only scoping while changing no actual behaviour. Removed.

      The raw audio socket (port 10000) is a plain TCP Socket, not part of the HTTP stack,
      so it is unaffected by this file regardless.

      system trust anchors are retained for ordinary public HTTPS (e.g. the GitHub release
      check); the child's own TLS uses certificate pinning, not these anchors.
    -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
